Z||000019||Nashco||10-13-2001||05:52 AM||||Ha..perfect timing, I'm finally getting stuck in GT3 again, now that school's done <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I was a huge GT2 freak, I loved that game more than air for a while.  My favorite car was the pikes peak cultus car (suzuki rally car).  The escudo was lighter/more powerful, but I could beat the computer up pikes peak with it, so why not go with the cheaper underdog car? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  There's not been anything I've come across in ANY race that the suzuki pikes peak car couldn't ANNIHILATE.  If you just love to win, that's your car.  It takes little driver skill to win, and hardly any to drive really good.  It's got monstrous spoilers, very light (accels and decels fast), all wheel drive, and still turns tight.  What more could you ask for?<P>There's not really much comparison with a fully modded regular car and a standard spec race car.  The race cars are pretty sweet, and if you max them out with power, tires, etc, they are awesome.  My latest fun car is the Mercedes touring car, I love it.  I love the looks more than anything...the vents on the top of the fenders look cool!  (By the way, for simple fiero...if you think the camaro is fast, get the corvette Z06!...max it out, and use very close gear ratios.  That car doesn't do well with tires, but it GOES for what you'd expect <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  And I liked the speedster best of all the mid-engine cars that were affordable at first.  It was a total b!tch to get the handling down, but if you learn to follow the right line, you didn't really need to use the brakes!)<P>I really liked the looks of the PT Spyder in GT2, that would be a sweet car, if they ever released something like that.  It was quite fiero/mr2-esque.  What I like most about GT3 is the details...the small detail in the sky, lights (shadows...lights throught THE TREES), when the car throws sparks from the ground, the red brakes, the obvious changes in ride height, etc.  I really like the data tracking function too...where you can watch replays, check out graphs, and make comparisons from a test run.  That is going to come in handy when I try to beat the game...it seems to be a lot tougher than GT2 because of the redesigned classes and such.  Has anybody tried Pro Vitz yet???  Man, that's tough!!!<P>My favorite tracks are Seattle (it's my hometown, I'm biased <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">), Laguna Seca, and Monaco now too.  Monaco is an awesome addition, but I miss Red Rock.  I've got Laguna Seca down to a T, and Seattle almost perfect.  I agree, the night course is tough, but I've almost got it fully memorized both directions.  That's the key...practice until you know the course, so you know exactly when to brake, the turns aren't hard, just there as reference points of when to slow down!  That's how I do things....<P>Lastly, one million credits is easy; once you have a fast, winning car, anyways...once you have that you don't need to spend on anything, and can just race.  Winning series races and endurance races are big money-makers because you get a car to sell if you don't want, and you get bonus for winning the series in addition to the race prize.  Also, you sometimes win a car that is better than your current winning car.  If this is the case, you can run even longer without spending.  Between not spending, earning, and selling cars...a million points isn't but a couple hours work, if that.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P><BR>||||reg||1
